Texas Obesity: 34.6%

CORRELATION between obesity and diabetes prevalence across states:
  Pearson r = 0.753  (95% CI 0.598 to 0.853),  p = 4.443e-10

LINEAR REGRESSION: diabetes prevalence = intercept + slope x obesity prevalence
  intercept = -0.812
  slope = 0.330 percentage points of diabetes per 1 percentage point of obesity
  R-squared = 0.567
  slope p-value = 4.443e-10
  slope 95% CI = 0.247 to 0.412

INTERPRETATION (plain English):
  States with higher obesity prevalence tend to have higher diabetes
  prevalence, and the association is strong and positive. This is an
  association between state-level averages, not proof that obesity
  causes diabetes in individuals, and not proof that changing a
  state's obesity level would change its diabetes level by the
  regression slope. Other state differences (age, income, health
  care, diet, physical activity) could explain part of the pattern.

